    <p class="title is-5 mathjax">
      
        Flow Autoencoders are Effective Protein Tokenizers
      
    </p>
    <p class="authors">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Authors:</span>
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Dilip%2C+R">Rohit Dilip</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Zhang%2C+E">Evan Zhang</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Varshney%2C+A">Ayush Varshney</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Van+Valen%2C+D">David Van Valen</a>
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="abstract mathjax">
      <span class="search-hit">Abstract</span>:
      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2510.00351v1-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        Protein structure tokenizers enable the creation of <span class="search-hit mathjax">multimodal</span> models of protein structure, sequence, and function. Current approaches to protein structure tokenization rely on bespoke components that are invariant to spatial symmetries, but that are challenging to optimize and scale. We present Kanzi, a flow-based tokenizer for tokenization and generation o&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2510.00351v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2510.00351v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2510.00351v1-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        Protein structure tokenizers enable the creation of <span class="search-hit mathjax">multimodal</span> models of protein structure, sequence, and function. Current approaches to protein structure tokenization rely on bespoke components that are invariant to spatial symmetries, but that are challenging to optimize and scale. We present Kanzi, a flow-based tokenizer for tokenization and generation of protein structures. Kanzi consists of a diffusion autoencoder trained with a flow matching loss. We show that this approach simplifies several aspects of protein structure tokenizers: frame-based representations can be replaced with global coordinates, complex losses are replaced with a single flow matching loss, and SE(3)-invariant attention operations can be replaced with standard attention. We find that these changes stabilize the training of parameter-efficient models that outperform existing tokenizers on reconstruction metrics at a fraction of the model size and training cost. An autoregressive model trained with Kanzi outperforms similar generative models that operate over tokens, although it does not yet match the performance of state-of-the-art continuous diffusion models. Code is available here: https://github.com/rdilip/kanzi/.
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2510.00351v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'none'; document.getElementById('2510.00351v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'inline';">&#9651; Less</a>
      </span>
    </p>
    

    <p class="is-size-7"><span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Submitted</span> 30 September, 2025; 
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">originally announced</span> October 2025.
      
    </p>
    

    

    
  </li>

  <li class="arxiv-result">
    <div class="is-marginless">
      <p class="list-title is-inline-block"><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00413">arXiv:2510.00413</a>
        <span>&nbsp;[<a href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.00413">pdf</a>, <a href="https://arxiv.org/ps/2510.00413">ps</a>, <a href="https://arxiv.org/format/2510.00413">other</a>]&nbsp;</span>
